\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The circle family}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{Fix #{m>0}. \ref{fdip-00BC} gives a probability law on the
dyadic index whose finite-dimensional distributions are the
Gaussian laws of the cyclic arc precision. \ref{fdip-00BE}
extends that law to a Hölder version on the circle of length
#{1}, of every order #{\gamma} with #{0\le\gamma<1/2}.
\ref{fdip-00AX} carries a uniform cycle law to another
uniform cycle law. \ref{fdip-00AY} preserves the shape
#{\cosh(m/N)} and its neighbor value, and a shape obeying the
same relation, with #{s_N-1} asymptotic to #{c/(2N^2)},
equals #{\cosh(m/N)} for #{c=m^2}. Equal arcs recover that
shape (\ref{fdip-00B7}).}

\p{A [finite-range circle precision](fdip-00C8) with one
second-order coefficient has Fourier moments and block means
that converge to those of one circle mesh. The mass squared
of that mesh is the ratio of the mass to the second-order
coefficient (\ref{fdip-00CC}, \ref{fdip-00CD}).}

\p{Other comparisons use a supplied circle. Block covariances
converge to an averaged Green function (\ref{fdip-00BP}),
while block maps and residue maps have different limits
(\ref{fdip-00BH}). The mesh interpolation converges strongly
to the resolvent of #{-d^2/dx^2+\mu} (\ref{fdip-00CG}).
Directed-cycle vertices lie at Gromov–Hausdorff distance at
most #{1/(2N)} from the circle (\ref{fdip-00CL}).}

\p{Spectral dimensions add on a product (\ref{fdip-00CQ}). A
bounded-degree uniform spectral gap leaves no positive plateau
(\ref{fdip-00CV}). The least positive eigenvalue of the cycle is
of order #{1/N^2} and tends to #{0} (\ref{fdip-00CS}).
\ref{fdip-00CT} gives the cycle heat trace a
plateau at #{1} on every window whose lower endpoint tends to
infinity, whose upper endpoint divided by #{N^2} tends to #{0},
and whose upper endpoint divided by the lower endpoint tends to
infinity. For a fixed positive integer #{n}, the product of
#{n} copies has a plateau at #{n}.}

\p{The increment metric is comparable to the square root of
arc length, and the two distances determine the same open
sets (\ref{fdip-00D2}). The increment metric and that square
root remain distinct functions of an arc (\ref{fdip-00D3}).
The increment metric has
Hausdorff dimension #{2} (\ref{fdip-00D7}), and arc length
has dimension #{1} (\ref{fdip-00D8}). On a unit cycle the
resistance between vertices separated by #{\ell} edges is
#{\ell(N-\ell)/N} (\ref{fdip-00DB}), and the conductance is
the reciprocal (\ref{fdip-00DM}). The covariance equals the
two-exponential kernel of \ref{fdip-00DX}.}
